The attached image demonstrates the active transport and cellular targeting of Nano-11 nanoparticles to draining lymph nodes. Flow cytometry plots show that Nano-11-containing myeloid cells peak at 3.5% on Day 2 post-injection, with migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) being the predominant cell type to internalize Nano-11. MigDCs also exhibit significantly higher mean fluorescence intensity (MFI) for Nano-11 compared to lymphoid tissue resident DCs (LTDCs) and monocytes/macrophages (Mo/MΦ), indicating a more efficient per-cell uptake by MigDCs.

The attached image can significantly contribute to applications in Cancer research. Nano-11, as an effective vaccine adjuvant that targets antigens to migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) and activates them, could be utilized in cancer immunotherapy. By co-delivering tumor-associated antigens (TAAs) or neoantigens with Nano-11, it could enhance antigen presentation by MigDCs in draining lymph nodes, thereby priming robust anti-tumor T-cell responses. This targeted delivery and strong immunostimulation could improve the efficacy of therapeutic cancer vaccines, leading to more effective elimination of cancer cells and durable anti-tumor immunity.